name: files
boss: CopyPasta
flavor: Every copy is a ghost of the original.
brief: Six commands move bytes around: cp copies, mv renames or moves, rm
brief: deletes, mkdir and rmdir make and unmake directories, touch creates an
brief: empty file or bumps its timestamp.
brief:
brief: Two of them are quietly dangerous. cp and mv overwrite the destination
brief: without asking. -n refuses to, -i asks first. Learn those two flags now
brief: and you will not need to learn them from a loss later.
---
q: {copy @cfg@ to @cfg2@|make a copy of @cfg@ named @cfg2@|duplicate @cfg@ as @cfg2@}
a: cp @cfg@ @cfg2@
state: exists:@cfg2@
ref: cp
teach: cp
hint: just cp, then the source, then the destination
---
q: {copy @txt@ into the @dir@ directory, keeping its name|put a copy of @txt@ inside @dir@ with the same name|copy @txt@ into @dir@ without renaming it}
a: cp @txt@ @dir@/
a: cp @txt@ @dir@
state: exists:@dir@/@txt@
ref: cp
teach: cp
hint: when the destination is a directory, cp keeps the original name for you
---
q: {copy the whole @dir@ directory, and everything inside it, to @dir2@-copy|copy @dir@ and all its contents to @dir2@-copy|duplicate @dir@, contents included, as @dir2@-copy}
a: cp -r @dir@ @dir2@-copy
a: cp -R @dir@ @dir2@-copy
a: cp -a @dir@ @dir2@-copy
state: exists:@dir2@-copy
ref: cp
teach: cp -r
hint: the flag cp calls "Recurse"
---
q: {copy @cfg@ to @cfg2@, but refuse to overwrite it if it already exists|copy @cfg@ to @cfg2@ without clobbering it if it's already there|copy @cfg@ to @cfg2@, only if @cfg2@ doesn't already exist}
a: cp -n @cfg@ @cfg2@
ref: cp
teach: cp -n
hint: n for "no clobber"
---
q: {copy @txt@ to @txt@.bak preserving its timestamps and permissions|copy @txt@ to @txt@.bak, keeping its timestamps and permissions intact|make a copy of @txt@ at @txt@.bak that keeps its timestamps and permissions}
a: cp -p @txt@ @txt@.bak
a: cp -a @txt@ @txt@.bak
state: exists:@txt@.bak
ref: cp
teach: cp -p
hint: p for preserve
---
q: {copy @dir@ to @dir2@-archive preserving everything -- one flag, not three|copy @dir@ to @dir2@-archive keeping everything about it intact, using a single flag|archive-copy @dir@ to @dir2@-archive, preserving everything with just one flag}
a: cp -a @dir@ @dir2@-archive
state: exists:@dir2@-archive
ref: cp
teach: cp -a
hint: cp calls it "Same as -dpR"
---
q: {rename @txt@ to @word@.txt|change @txt@'s name to @word@.txt|give @txt@ the new name @word@.txt}
a: mv @txt@ @word@.txt
state: exists:@word@.txt
ref: mv
teach: mv
hint: mv renames when the destination isn't a directory
---
q: {move @cfg@ into @dir@/|relocate @cfg@ into the @dir@ directory|put @cfg@ inside @dir@}
a: mv @cfg@ @dir@/
a: mv @cfg@ @dir@
state: exists:@dir@/@cfg@
ref: mv
teach: mv
hint: same command as renaming -- the destination just happens to be a directory
---
q: {move @txt@ to @dir@/, but never overwrite a file already there|move @txt@ into @dir@ without clobbering anything already there|relocate @txt@ to @dir@, refusing if the name is already taken}
a: mv -n @txt@ @dir@/
a: mv -n @txt@ @dir@
ref: mv
teach: mv -n
hint: n for no-clobber, same idea as cp -n
---
q: {delete @txt@|remove @txt@|get rid of @txt@}
a: rm @txt@
state: gone:@txt@
ref: rm
teach: rm
hint: just rm and the filename -- no flags for a plain file
---
q: {delete @dir@ and everything inside it|remove @dir@ along with everything it contains|wipe out @dir@ and its contents}
a: rm -r @dir@
a: rm -rf @dir@
state: gone:@dir@
ref: rm
teach: rm -r
hint: r for recursive -- required before rm will touch a directory's contents
---
q: {delete @txt@ without complaining if it is already gone|remove @txt@ silently even if it doesn't exist|delete @txt@, staying quiet if it's already missing}
a: rm -f @txt@
ref: rm
teach: rm -f
hint: f for force -- it also silences the "no such file" error
---
q: %make% a directory called @word@
a: mkdir @word@
state: exists:@word@
ref: mkdir
teach: mkdir
hint: just mkdir and the name -- no flags needed
---
q: %make% @dir@/@word@/deep, making the missing parent directories too
a: mkdir -p @dir@/@word@/deep
state: exists:@dir@/@word@/deep
ref: mkdir
teach: mkdir -p
hint: p for parents
---
q: %make% a directory @word@ that only you can enter, using a numeric mode
a: mkdir -m 700 @word@
state: exists:@word@
ref: mkdir
teach: mkdir -m
hint: -m sets permissions at creation time -- 700 is owner-only rwx
---
q: {remove the empty directory @word@ -- create it first if you like|delete the empty directory @word@, making one first if you need to|get rid of the empty directory @word@, creating it beforehand if you like}
a: rmdir @word@
ref: rmdir
teach: rmdir
hint: rmdir only works when the directory is empty -- rm -r doesn't care
---
q: %make% an empty file called @word@.txt
a: touch @word@.txt
a: > @word@.txt
state: exists:@word@.txt
ref: touch
teach: touch
hint: touch creates it if missing, or just bumps its timestamp if it exists
---
q: {update the timestamp on @txt@ to now, without changing its contents|bump @txt@'s modification time to now, leaving its contents alone|refresh @txt@'s timestamp without touching what's inside it}
a: touch @txt@
state: match:@txt@:rotate
ref: touch
teach: touch
hint: with no flags, touch just sets the timestamp to now
---
q: {update @txt@'s timestamp but do NOT create it if it is missing|bump @txt@'s timestamp, but skip creating it if it doesn't exist|refresh @txt@'s timestamp only if it already exists}
a: touch -c @txt@
ref: touch
teach: touch -c
hint: c stops touch from creating the file when it doesn't exist
---
q: %make% a symbolic link called @word@-link pointing at @txt@
a: ln -s @txt@ @word@-link
state: exists:@word@-link
ref: ln
teach: ln -s
hint: s for symbolic; without it you get a hard link
---
q: %make% a hard link called @word@-hard to @txt@
a: ln @txt@ @word@-hard
state: exists:@word@-hard
ref: ln
teach: ln
hint: no flag needed -- ln makes a hard link by default
---
q: %make% a symlink @word@-link to @txt@, replacing it if it already exists
a: ln -sf @txt@ @word@-link
a: ln -fs @txt@ @word@-link
state: exists:@word@-link
ref: ln
teach: ln -f
hint: combine -s for symbolic with -f to replace what's already there
---
q: {copy @cfg@ to @cfg2@, asking before it would overwrite anything|copy @cfg@ to @cfg2@, but prompt first if that would overwrite something|copy @cfg@ over @cfg2@, confirming before any overwrite}
a: cp -i @cfg@ @cfg2@
ref: cp
teach: cp -i
hint: i for interactive -- prompts before clobbering
---
q: {copy @cfg@ over @cfg2@ forcing it through even if the target resists|copy @cfg@ onto @cfg2@, forcing past anything that would block it|force a copy of @cfg@ over @cfg2@ no matter what's in the way}
a: cp -f @cfg@ @cfg2@
state: exists:@cfg2@
ref: cp
teach: cp -f
hint: f removes the destination first if a plain overwrite would fail
---
q: {move @txt@ to @word@.txt, asking first if that would overwrite something|move @txt@ to @word@.txt, but prompt before overwriting anything|rename @txt@ to @word@.txt, confirming first if it would clobber something}
a: mv -i @txt@ @word@.txt
ref: mv
teach: mv -i
hint: i for interactive, same idea as cp -i
---
q: {move @txt@ to @word@.txt, forcing it without any prompt|move @txt@ to @word@.txt, overwriting anything there with no confirmation|force the move of @txt@ to @word@.txt, skipping any prompt}
a: mv -f @txt@ @word@.txt
state: exists:@word@.txt
ref: mv
teach: mv -f
hint: f skips confirmation -- the opposite of -i
---
q: {delete @txt@ but ask for confirmation first|remove @txt@, prompting for confirmation first|delete @txt@ only after you confirm it}
a: rm -i @txt@
ref: rm
teach: rm -i
hint: i for interactive -- prompts before removing
---
q: {remove @dir@/@word@/deep and any parent directories left empty behind it|delete @dir@/@word@/deep, then clean up any now-empty parents above it|remove @dir@/@word@/deep along with the empty parent directories it leaves behind}
a: mkdir -p @dir@/@word@/deep && rmdir -p @dir@/@word@/deep
ref: rmdir
teach: rmdir -p
hint: p walks back up removing each parent as long as it's now empty
---
q: %make% a named pipe called @word@.fifo
a: mkfifo @word@.fifo
state: exists:@word@.fifo
ref: mkfifo
teach: mkfifo
hint: a fifo connects a writer to a reader instead of storing data
---
q: %make% a named pipe readable and writable only by you
a: mkfifo -m 600 @word@.fifo
state: exists:@word@.fifo
ref: mkfifo
teach: mkfifo -m
hint: -m sets permissions at creation, same as mkdir -m
---
q: %make% a temporary file with a name nothing else will collide with
a: mktemp
out: ^/
ref: mktemp
teach: mktemp
hint: generates and creates a uniquely-named file, then prints its path
---
q: %make% a temporary directory rather than a file
a: mktemp -d
out: ^/
ref: mktemp
teach: mktemp -d
hint: d for directory instead of a file
---
q: %make% a temporary name without actually creating anything
a: mktemp -u
out: ^/
ref: mktemp
teach: mktemp -u
hint: u for unsafe -- hands you a name without reserving it, so something else could grab it first
---
q: %make% a temporary file, staying silent if it cannot
a: mktemp -q
out: ^/
ref: mktemp
teach: mktemp -q
hint: q suppresses the error message on failure
